Negative control — the display strings the editor KEEPS after a settled save
are composed from the SAVED model, not from the form-open snapshot (review
blocker B1, meta half).

Points this module's `setMeta` at `baseModel` instead of `model`, leaving the
model itself entirely correct. `baseModel` is the clone taken when the form
opened and legitimately never moves (parity L3), so every derivation that reads
it reports the pre-edit address forever.

WHY THIS TARGET, AND NOT THE `setModel` FOLD IT REPLACES
The mutant this file previously carried dropped `...deriveMeta(model)` from
`setModel`. It was written against the tree in which a settled save left the
machine's meta un-re-derived, and it flipped NOTHING once B1's root cause was
closed: the shared `dataManagerMachine` takes `processed -> available` under
`allowMultipleEdits` (which this manager always sets), re-enters
`available.checking.parsing`, and runs `setMeta` on its `onDone`. With the
`parse` service no longer re-deriving the model from `baseModel` on that
data-less `xstate.after(wait)` re-entry, that `setMeta` now sees the SAVED model
and publishes the right strings — with or without the `setModel` fold. Measured:
whole module suite byte-identical to baseline under the old mutant. A control
that cannot go red is not a control, so the target moved to the derivation that
actually feeds the read-back.

(The `setModel` fold is still load-bearing for the ~10ms window at `processed`
and for any consumer running with `allowMultipleEdits` false. Nothing in this
suite samples that window, so it is not what this control measures.)

The mutant is invisible at every gate that reads the MODEL. The PUT is correct,
the server is correct, the save resolves the saved address, `useContext().model`
carries the new values, and `isDirty` is still true. Only the display strings
the manager publishes alongside them are the pre-save ones.

Declared subject — the B1 settled meta read-back. Open an EXISTING address,
change a field the meta composes (`name`, or any of address1 / address2 / city /
state / postcode), save, wait for the save's own refetch to land, and assert on
`useContext()`:
  - `description` reflects the SAVED field order
  - `title` reflects the SAVED name/address1
Under the mutant both report the form-open address.

What does NOT discriminate:
  - asserting the value the save RESOLVES. That value is `context.model`, and
    `title` / `description` ride on it from `mapAddress`, not from `deriveMeta`
    — the resolved read-back stays green under this mutant. The assertion MUST
    be taken off `useContext()`.
  - asserting `title` / `description` after `input()` / a plain `SET` with no
    save. Under the mutant those report `baseModel` too, but so would a form
    that was merely never edited — it does not localise to the save.
  - asserting `useContext().model` or `isDirty` — unchanged by the mutant.

Measured blast radius, suite at this commit: ONE test — the declared subject
("AC-23/AC-17 keeps a description composed from the SAVED address once the
refetch has landed"). 2 failed / 119 passed of 121, the second failure being the
standing AC-20/F6 halt, which fails identically without the mutant. The mutated
tree type-checks (exit 0), so the red can only come from the read-back.

This is the complement of
`client-address.saved-model-shape.must-fail.patch`: that one reverts the model
(and the meta with it, since the meta is derived from the model — measured, it
flips four tests including this one); this one leaves the model correct and
moves only the derivation's source. A red in both localises to the model; a red
HERE alone localises to the meta.

